<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maeda Toshiie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1538">1538</a>–<a href="/years/1599">1599</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maeda Toshiie was a Japanese warlord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> and founder of the Kaga domain. A companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and later a leading general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, he received northern Kaga in 1583 and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> his castle town. He served as one of Hideyoshi's Five Elders, and his family became the richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie.jpg" alt="Maeda Toshiie" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maeda_Toshiie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maeda Toshiie was born on 15 January 1538 in the village of Arako, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, the fourth of seven brothers of Maeda Toshimasa, who held Arako Castle. His childhood name was Inuchiyo. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> from childhood, first as a page, and like Nobunaga dressed in the outlandish style of a kabukimono. His loyalty was rewarded when he was allowed to head the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a>, unusual for a fourth son. His preferred weapon was the yari, a spear, and he became known as Yari no Mataza. He is believed to have been a friend of Kinoshita Tokichiro, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, in their youth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Toshiie began in Nobunaga's personal unit, the akahoro-shū, whose members wore a red cloak, and later led it and became an infantry captain. In 1559 he killed Nobunaga's attendant Jūami in a quarrel over a stolen hairpin and was dismissed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a>. He fought secretly at Okehazama in 1560 and at Moribe in 1561, and after Moribe Nobunaga pardoned him. In 1569 Nobunaga ordered his elder brother Toshihisa, who was in frail health, to give up the headship, and Toshiie succeeded. He fought at Anegawa, Nagashino and Tedorigawa, and was granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Fuchū and a domain spanning Noto and Kaga, which became the richest han of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, rated at a million koku.</p><p>After Nobunaga's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> in 1582 Toshiie first backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a>, and in 1583 he changed sides to Hideyoshi during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Battle of Shizugatake</a>. In 1584 he defeated his friend Sassa Narimasa at Suemori Castle. In 1590 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu">Uesugi Kagekatsu</a> led a force of 35,000 through the Usui Pass in the Odawara Campaign, taking a series of Hōjō strongholds, and in 1591 he helped suppress the Kunohe rebellion. In 1587 he sheltered the Christian daimyo Dom Justo Takayama.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before dying in 1598 Hideyoshi named Toshiie to the council of Five Elders to support his heir Toyotomi Hideyori. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> arranged forbidden marriage alliances, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> and others relied on Toshiie and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> supported him, so Ieyasu apologised. Toshiie died on 27 April 1599, himself ailing. His son Toshinaga was then accused by Ieyasu of an assassination plot, and Toshiie's widow Matsu persuaded the clan to submit by going as a hostage to the Tokugawa; the Maeda fought on the Tokugawa side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600. A sword, the Ōdenta, once held by the Ashikaga shoguns, passed to Toshiie, and a legend says it healed his daughter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maeda Toshiie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maeda Toshiie</b>, a comrade of Oda Nobunaga and Toyotomi Hideyoshi, received northern Kaga after the defeat of Shibata Katsuie in 1583 and moved into Kanazawa castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-domain">Kaga Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.18,136.91&amp;year=1577" aria-label="Nagoya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maeda Toshiie"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4093 4505h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4093 4505h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.18,136.91&amp;year=1577">What was here in 1577? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
